question:
Given positive integers $m$ and $n \geq m$, determine the largest number of dominoes $(1 \times 2$ or $2 \times 1$ rectangles) that can be placed on a rectangular board with $m$ rows and $2 n$ columns consisting of cells $(1 \times 1$ squares $)$ so that:



(i) each domino covers exactly two adjacent cells of the board;



(ii) no two dominoes overlap;



(iii) no two form a $2 \times 2$ square; and



(iv) the bottom row of the board is completely covered by $n$ dominoes.

response:
The bottom row can be covered by $n$ dominoes in only two ways, as shown in the figure below.

[asy]
size(2cm);
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,2)--(0,2)--cycle);
draw((0,1)--(2,1));
draw((0.5,0)--(1.5,0));
draw((0.5,0)--(0.5,1));
draw((1.5,0)--(1.5,1));
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1,1)--(0,1)--cycle);
draw((1,0)--(2,0)--(2,1)--(1,1)--cycle);
label("$A$", (0.5,0), S);
label("$B$", (1.5,0), S);
[/asy]

Let us color the cells of the board as shown in the figure below.

[asy]
unitsize(0.7cm);
for (int j = 1; j <= 2; ++j)
{
  for (int i = 1; i <= 2; ++i)
  {
    draw((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle);
    if (i == 1)
    {
      fill((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle,gray(0.7));
      draw((j-1,0)--(j-1,1));
    }
  }
}
for (int j = 3; j <= 2*2; j+=2)
{
  for (int i = 1; i <= 2; ++i)
  {
    draw((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle);
    if (i == 1)
    {
      fill((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle,gray(0.7));
      draw((j-1,0)--(j-1,1));
    }
  }
}
for (int j = 1; j <= 2*m; j+=2)
{
  for (int i = 2; i <= m; ++i)
  {
    draw((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle);
    if (j == 2)
    {
      fill((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle,gray(0.7));
      draw((j-1,0)--(j-1,1));
    }
    else
    {
      fill((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle,gray(0.7));
      draw((j-1,0)--(j-1,1));
      draw((j-1,1)--(j,1));
    }
  }
}
[/asy]

We see that each domino covers one white cell and one black cell.  Thus, the number of dominoes is equal to the number of white cells.  Also, by condition (iii), no two dominoes can cover the four cells of a $2 \times 2$ square, so the number of dominoes is at most the number of white cells that are not in a $2 \times 2$ square.  We denote this number by $W$.

Clearly, $W = n + (m - 1)n - (m - 2)n = 2n - 1$.  The number of dominoes is equal to $2n - 1$ if and only if every domino covers a white cell that is not in a $2 \times 2$ square.  Such a placement is shown in the figure below.

[asy]
unitsize(0.7cm);
for (int j = 1; j <= 2; ++j)
{
  for (int i = 1; i <= 2; ++i)
  {
    draw((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle);
    if (i == 1)
    {
      fill((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle,gray(0.7));
      draw((j-1,0)--(j-1,1));
    }
  }
}
for (int j = 3; j <= 2*2; j+=2)
{
  for (int i = 1; i <= 2; ++i)
  {
    draw((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle);
    if (i == 1)
    {
      fill((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle,gray(0.7));
      draw((j-1,0)--(j-1,1));
    }
  }
}
for (int j = 1; j <= 2*m; j+=2)
{
  for (int i = 2; i <= m; ++i)
  {
    draw((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle);
    if (j == 2)
    {
      fill((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle,gray(0.7));
      draw((j-1,0)--(j-1,1));
    }
    else
    {
      fill((j-1,0)--(j,0)--(j,1)--(j-1,1)--cycle,gray(0.7));
      draw((j-1,0)--(j-1,1));
      draw((j-1,1)--(j,1));
    }
  }
}
[/asy]

Thus, the largest number of dominoes is $\boxed{2n - 1}$.
</think>

<answer>\boxed{2n - 1}</answer>

question:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R}^{+} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{+}$ such that

$$
f(x+f(y))=f(x+y)+f(y)\tag{1}
$$

for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}^{+}$. (Symbol $\mathbb{R}^{+}$denotes the set of all positive real numbers.)

response:
The inequality is equivalent to
\begin{align*}
x^{2 N}+1 &\leqslant 2\left(a_{n}(x-1)^{2}+x\right)^{N} \\
&=2\left(a_{n}(x-1)^{2}+x\right)^{N}
\end{align*}
For this to hold for all $x$, it is necessary that the two sides of this inequality be equal for $x=1$. Thus, $2=2 a_{n}^{N}$ and $a_{n}=2^{-1/N}$. Substituting this into the original inequality, we get
\begin{align*}
x^{2 N}+1 &\leqslant 2\left(2^{-1/N}(x-1)^{2}+x\right)^{N} \\
\frac{x^{2 N}+1}{2} &\leqslant\left(2^{-1/N}(x-1)^{2}+x\right)^{N} \\
\frac{x^{2 N}+1}{2} &\leqslant\left(2^{-1/N}(x-1)^{2}+x\right)^{N}
\end{align*}
Let $f(x)=2^{-1/N}(x-1)^{2}+x$ and $g(x)=\sqrt[N]{\frac{x^{2 N}+1}{2}}$. Note that $f(x)=x$ for $x=1$. Since $g(x)$ is a convex function, it is sufficient to show that $f(x)$ is tangent to $g(x)$ at $x=1$. We have
\begin{align*}
f(1) &=1 \\
g(1) &=1 \\
f^{\prime}(1) &=1 \\
g^{\prime}(1) &=1
\end{align*}
Therefore, $f(x)$ is tangent to $g(x)$ at $x=1$. Since $g(x)$ is a convex function, we have $g(x)\geqslant f(x)$ for all $x$. Thus, the smallest value of $a_{n}$ is $2^{-1/N}=\boxed{\sqrt[N]{\frac{1}{2}}}$.
</think>

<answer>\boxed{\sqrt[N]{\frac{1}{2}}}</answer>

Since $B P T Q$ is a parallelogram, we have $B Q = P T$ and $B P = Q T$. Since $B Q P M$ is a cyclic quadrilateral, $\angle M B P = \angle M Q P$ and $\angle P M B = \angle P Q B$. Therefore, $\triangle B P M \cong \triangle Q T P$, and thus $B M = P T$. Hence, $B M = Q T$. Since $B Q T M$ is an isosceles trapezoid, it is also a cyclic quadrilateral, and so $\angle T M B = \angle T Q B$.

[asy]
import graph;
size(200);

pair A,B,C,M,P,Q,T;

A = (1,0);
B = (0,2);
C = (-1,0);
M = (A + C) / 2;
