label("$B$",B,N);
label("$C$",C,SW);
label("$M$",M,NE);
label("$P$",P,NW);
label("$Q$",Q,SW);
label("$T$",T,N);
[/asy]

Since $\triangle A B C$ is acute, $T$ must be on the arc $A C$ not containing $B$. Then $\angle B A C = \angle B T C$. But $\angle B T C = \angle B M P$, and so $\angle B A C = \angle B M P$. Therefore, $\triangle A B M \sim \triangle M B P$, and so
\[\frac{B M}{A B} = \frac{B P}{B M}.\]Since $B M = P T$ and $B P = Q T$, we have
\[\frac{B M}{A B} = \frac{Q T}{B M}.\]Since $B Q T M$ is an isosceles trapezoid, $B Q = P T = B M$, and so
\[\frac{B M}{A B} = \frac{B M}{B Q}.\]Therefore, $A B = B Q$, and so $\triangle A B Q$ is isosceles. Then $\angle A B Q = 2 \angle B A Q = 2 \angle B T Q = 2 \angle T Q B = \angle T M B$. Since $\triangle A B M \sim \triangle M B P$, we have $\angle M B P = \angle M A B = \angle B A C$. Therefore, $\angle A B Q = \angle M B P + \angle P B Q = \angle B A C + \angle P B Q = \angle T M B$. Hence, $\angle P B Q = \angle B A C$.

Since $\angle P B Q$ and $\angle B A C$ subtend the same arc $B C$, we have $\angle P B Q = \angle B A C$. Therefore, $P B Q M$ is a cyclic quadrilateral, and so $\angle B Q M = \angle B P M = \angle B A M$. Therefore, $\angle B Q M = \angle B A Q$. Hence, $\triangle A B Q \sim \triangle M B Q$, and so
\[\frac{B Q}{B M} = \frac{A B}{M B}.\]Therefore, $B Q^2 = A B \cdot B M$. Since $A B = B Q$, we have $B M = A B$. Hence, $B T = 2 B M$.
