Suppose Horst always places a black knight on a square with the same color as the previous square. Since a knight always moves to a square of a different color, this strategy will work.

Now, we consider two cases:

Case 1: The game ends with a white queen.

In this case, the last move was by Horst. Since the game ended, there must be no empty square of the same color as the last black knight. Since the previous black knight was placed on a square of the same color as the one before, and so on, there must be no empty square of the same color as the first black knight.

Since every knight attacks 8 squares of a different color, and there are 200 squares of each color, there can be at most $\left\lfloor \frac{200}{8+1}\right\rfloor = 22$ knights of the same color. Therefore, there are at most 44 knights in total.

Case 2: The game ends with a black knight.

In this case, the last move was by Queenie. Since the game ended, there must be no empty square of the same color as the last white queen. Since the previous white queen was placed on a square of the same color as the one before, and so on, there must be no empty square of the same color as the first white queen.

Since every knight attacks 8 squares of a different color, and there are 200 squares of each color, there can be at most $\left\lfloor \frac{200}{8+1}\right\rfloor = 22$ knights of the same color. Therefore, there are at most 44 knights in total.

Therefore, the maximal positive $K$ such that, regardless of the strategy of Queenie, Horst can put at least $K$ knights on the board is $\boxed{22}$.
